description:
## Problem
Job manager uses synchronous file operations that block the event loop:
- `fs.writeFileSync()` in saveToDisk()
- `fs.readFileSync()` in loadFromDisk()
- `fs.existsSync()` checks

## Files Affected
- `backend/src/services/jobManager.ts` (lines 62-80, 118-141)

## Solution
Convert all sync operations to async:

```typescript
private async loadFromDiskAsync(): Promise<void> {
  try {
    await fs.promises.access(this.dataFilePath);
    const data = await fs.promises.readFile(this.dataFilePath, 'utf-8');
    // parse and load...
  } catch (error) {
    if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== 'ENOENT') {
      log.error({ err: error }, 'Failed to load jobs from disk');
    }
  }
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Convert loadFromDisk to async
- [ ] Convert saveToDisk to async  
- [ ] Update constructor to use async initialization
- [ ] Remove all fs.*Sync calls
- [ ] Add tests for async persistence

## Problem
Logo processing code is nearly identical in two places:
- `backend/src/services/exportService.ts` (lines 486-682) - logo export
- `backend/src/services/importService.ts` (lines 800-900+) - logo import

Both have similar patterns for:
- URL vs file-based logo detection
- File extension handling
- Checksum calculation
- Error handling

## Solution
Extract common logo utilities:

```typescript
// backend/src/utils/logoUtils.ts
export function isExternalUrl(url: string | undefined): boolean;
export function getLogoContentType(ext: string): string;
export function calculateLogoChecksum(buffer: Buffer): string;
export interface LogoMetadata {
  name: string;
  ext: string;
  isUrl: boolean;
  url?: string;
  sourceId?: number;
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Create logoUtils.ts with shared functions
- [ ] Update exportService to use shared utils
- [ ] Update importService to use shared utils
- [ ] Update simpleLogoImport to use shared utils
- [ ] Add unit tests for logo utilities

## Problem
Connection validation logic is duplicated in multiple route files:
- `backend/src/routes/export.ts` (line 29)
- `backend/src/routes/import.ts` (line 113)
- `backend/src/routes/sync.ts` (line 95)

Each has similar checks for url, username, password.

## Solution
Create a shared validation function or middleware:
```typescript
// backend/src/utils/validation.ts
export function validateDispatcharrConnection(
  conn: DispatcharrConnection,
  label: string
): void {
  if (!conn?.url || !conn?.username || !conn?.password) {
    throw new ValidationError(
      `Invalid ${label} connection: url, username, and password are required`
    );
  }
}
```

Or use Zod schema validation that already exists in schemas/.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Create shared validateDispatcharrConnection() function
- [ ] Update export route to use shared validation
- [ ] Update import route to use shared validation
- [ ] Update sync route to use shared validation
- [ ] Add unit tests for validation function

## Problem
The `getAllPaginated()` method in exportService.ts and importService.ts has no maximum iteration limit. If an API returns malformed responses like `{results: [], next: true}` forever, it will loop infinitely.

## Files Affected
- `backend/src/services/exportService.ts` (lines 31-58)
- `backend/src/services/importService.ts` (lines 133-170)

## Solution
Add a MAX_PAGES constant and iteration counter:
```typescript
const MAX_PAGES = 10000;
let pageCount = 0;
while (true) {
  if (pageCount++ > MAX_PAGES) {
    log.warn({ endpoint }, 'Max pages exceeded, stopping pagination');
    break;
  }
  // existing logic...
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Add MAX_PAGES constant to constants.ts
- [ ] Update getAllPaginated() in exportService.ts
- [ ] Update getAllPaginated() in importService.ts
- [ ] Log warning when max pages exceeded
- [ ] Add unit test for max page limit

## Problem
Over 20 instances of `any` type used throughout services, breaking TypeScript's type safety:
- `backend/src/services/exportService.ts`: Lines 31, 32, 103, 429
- `backend/src/services/importService.ts`: Lines 138-140, 1099, 1138
- `backend/src/services/notificationService.ts`: Line 473

This reduces IDE support and makes refactoring dangerous.

## Solution
Replace `any` with proper types:

1. Add generic type parameter to getAllPaginated:
```typescript
private async getAllPaginated<T>(
  client: DispatcharrClient,
  endpoint: string,
  jobId?: string
): Promise<T[]>
```

2. Type exportData properly:
```typescript
interface ExportData {
  exported_at: string;
  source_url: string;
  data: {
    channelGroups?: ChannelGroup[];
    channels?: Channel[];
    // etc
  };
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Add generic type to getAllPaginated in both services
- [ ] Create ExportData interface
- [ ] Create ImportData interface
- [ ] Replace all any[] with proper types
- [ ] Run type-check to verify no regressions

## Problem
The `saveToDisk()` method in jobManager.ts uses `fs.writeFileSync()` which blocks the event loop. In high-concurrency scenarios with many simultaneous job updates, this causes:
1. Event loop blocking
2. Potential race conditions when multiple jobs update at once
3. Data loss if writes interleave incorrectly

## Files Affected
- `backend/src/services/jobManager.ts` (lines 118-141)

## Solution
1. Switch to async file operations with `fs.promises.writeFile()`
2. Add a write queue/debounce to batch updates
3. Use atomic write pattern (write to temp, then rename)

```typescript
private saveQueue: Promise<void> = Promise.resolve();

private async saveToDiskAsync(): Promise<void> {
  this.saveQueue = this.saveQueue.then(async () => {
    const tempPath = `${this.dataFilePath}.tmp`;
    await fs.promises.writeFile(tempPath, JSON.stringify(data, null, 2));
    await fs.promises.rename(tempPath, this.dataFilePath);
  });
  return this.saveQueue;
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Convert saveToDisk to async
- [ ] Implement write queue to serialize writes
- [ ] Use atomic write pattern (temp file + rename)
- [ ] Update all callers to await save
- [ ] Add tests for concurrent job updates

## Problem
The import route uses `multer.memoryStorage()` with a 10GB limit. For large backup files, this loads the entire file into RAM, which can cause OOM errors.

## Files Affected
- `backend/src/routes/import.ts` (lines 16-21)

## Current Code
```typescript
const upload = multer({
  storage: multer.memoryStorage(),
  limits: { fileSize: 10 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024 }
});
```

## Solution
Use disk storage with streaming:
```typescript
const upload = multer({
  storage: multer.diskStorage({
    destination: './temp/uploads',
    filename: (req, file, cb) => {
      cb(null, `${Date.now()}-${file.originalname}`);
    }
  }),
  limits: { fileSize: 10 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024 }
});
```

Then read from disk in the handler and clean up after processing.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Switch multer to disk storage
- [ ] Update import handler to read from disk
- [ ] Clean up temp files after import completes
- [ ] Clean up temp files on import failure
- [ ] Add temp file cleanup to startup routine
